  "statement": "You are given `n` `points` in the plane that are all **distinct**, where `points[i] = [x_(i), y_(i)]`. A **boomerang** is a tuple of points `(i, j, k)` such that the distance between `i` and `j` equals the distance between `i` and `k` **(the order of the tuple matters)**.\n\nReturn _the number of boomerangs_.\n\n**Example 1:**\n\n```\nInput: points = [[0,0],[1,0],[2,0]]\nOutput: 2\nExplanation: The two boomerangs are [[1,0],[0,0],[2,0]] and [[1,0],[2,0],[0,0]].\n```\n\n**Example 2:**\n\n```\nInput: points = [[1,1],[2,2],[3,3]]\nOutput: 2\n```\n\n**Example 3:**\n\n```\nInput: points = [[1,1]]\nOutput: 0\n```\n\n**Constraints:**\n\n*   `n == points.length`\n*   `1 <= n <= 500`\n*   `points[i].length == 2`\n*   `-10^(4) <= x_(i), y_(i) <= 10^(4)`\n*   All the points are **unique**.",
  "constraints": [
    "n == points.length",
    "1 <= n <= 500",
    "points[i].length == 2",
    "-104 <= xi, yi <= 104",
    "All the points are unique."
  ],
  "examples": [
